1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
What if an AI could answer your phone calls,

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:05,500
understand who's calling,

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:09,200
and actually have a conversation on your behalf?

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:11,700
That's the idea we're chasing.

5
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:15,600
And on day one, we hit a wall.

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,500
First: what can't be done.

7
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:20,400
The obvious plan?

8
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:22,800
Build it inside an Android app.

9
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:25,300
Detect an incoming SIM call,

10
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:27,200
answer it automatically,

11
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:29,900
capture both sides of the conversation,

12
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:32,800
send that audio to an AI model,

13
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:36,400
and play the reply back to the caller.

14
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,000
On paper, it sounds simple.

15
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,200
Android does give developers call APIs.

16
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:46,100
CallScreeningService lets an app identify an incoming call

17
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:49,400
and allow, silence, or reject it.

18
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:52,600
InCallService gives a default dialer its call interface

19
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,700
and call management.

20
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:57,900
But neither one hands a normal app the actual audio

21
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:00,800
of a cellular call.

22
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:04,500
Android does define audio sources for exactly that:

23
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:07,700
VOICE_UPLINK, VOICE_DOWNLINK, VOICE_CALL.

24
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:10,200
But each one requires a permission called

25
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,600
CAPTURE_AUDIO_OUTPUT,

26
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,600
and that permission is reserved for system components.

27
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:18,700
It is not available to third-party apps.

28
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:21,000
There was a workaround.

29
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,300
Android 10 blocked call recording by default,

30
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:26,600
so apps started using the Accessibility Service

31
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:28,700
to get around it.

32
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:32,800
And in 2022, Google Play shut that door too.

33
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:35,700
Its policy says the Accessibility API

34
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:38,400
is not for recording call audio.

35
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:40,900
Google Play also restricts the call log

36
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:42,800
and SMS permissions.

37
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:45,400
Only apps that are the default phone, SMS,

38
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:48,300
or assistant app can use them.

39
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:51,200
Then India adds another layer.

40
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,500
TRAI's rules for commercial communication require businesses

41
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:57,800
to register on the DLT platform and to use designated

42
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:00,000
number series:

43
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,800
140 for promotional calls,

44
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:06,100
1600 for service and transactional calls.

45
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:09,100
Ordinary 10-digit numbers are out.

46
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:11,900
Those rules are aimed at commercial calling,

47
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:14,200
but they show how closely India watches numbers,

48
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,500
telecom resources, and automated voices.

49
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:20,800
So this isn't a bug waiting for a clever fix.

50
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:22,800
It's a boundary

51
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:25,800
set by the operating system, the app store,

52
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:28,800
and the telecom network itself.

53
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:32,000
Now: what can be done, today.

54
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,800
If the audio can't come out of the phone,

55
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,200
the call has to arrive somewhere else.

56
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:40,500
An authorized cloud telephony provider can receive the call

57
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:43,000
on its own infrastructure.

58
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,900
The owner's number can forward calls to it,

59
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:48,300
for example when they're busy, unanswered,

60
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:50,400
or unreached.

61
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:53,400
Take Exotel, an Indian cloud telephony provider.

62
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:55,700
When a call is answered,

63
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:58,600
its AgentStream service opens a WebSocket

64
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:00,800
to your server

65
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:03,800
and streams the caller's audio as raw PCM,

66
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:07,000
in chunks of about 100 milliseconds.

67
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,800
It's telephone-quality audio: 8 kHz.

68
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:13,000
With its VoiceBot setup, the connection is two-way.

69
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,700
Your server sends audio back on the same socket,

70
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:18,000
and the caller hears it.

71
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,500
And here's a detail that matters:

72
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:23,100
the provider only relays the audio.

73
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,900
It does not do speech recognition

74
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:28,200
or voice generation.

75
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:30,900
That part is ours.

76
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:33,900
So the pipeline has three jobs.

77
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:36,400
First: speech-to-text.

78
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:39,500
A streaming recognizer turns the caller's audio into text

79
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:41,900
while they're still talking.

80
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:45,300
Sarvam offers this with its Saaras models,

81
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:47,800
built for Indian languages with support

82
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:51,500
for switching between languages like Hindi and English.

83
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:54,200
Second: the language model.

84
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:56,900
It takes the transcript and the conversation so far,

85
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:59,300
works out what the caller wants,

86
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:01,800
decides what to say,

87
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:05,300
and can call authorized tools, like a calendar.

88
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:07,800
Third: text-to-speech.

89
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:10,700
Bulbul from Sarvam, or ElevenLabs,

90
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:13,300
turns the reply into audio that goes straight back

91
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:15,600
down the same connection.

92
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:19,200
Wiring all of this together is the orchestration layer.

93
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:21,500
Vapi is a hosted platform

94
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:23,800
where you plug in your own speech, language,

95
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:25,900
and voice providers,

96
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:28,400
and it handles turn-taking, interruptions,

97
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:30,600
and tool calls.

98
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:32,800
Or you build it yourself

99
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:35,700
with open-source frameworks like Pipecat

100
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:38,200
or a custom Node.js layer.

101
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:41,700
Sarvam's own documentation even walks through a phone voice agent

102
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:45,400
built from Exotel, Pipecat, and Sarvam.

103
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:49,700
But a working pipeline is not a good phone agent.

104
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:52,900
People leave only a fraction of a second between turns.

105
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:55,700
Every step in this chain—the network,

106
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:59,600
the recognizer, the model, the voice—adds delay.

107
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:02,800
Interruptions—what engineers call "barge-in"—

108
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:05,900
mean the AI must stop talking the instant the caller starts.

109
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:09,100
Phone lines are noisy and narrow. Connections drop.

110
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:12,100
Context has to survive the whole call,

111
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:15,300
and the AI has to know when to step back

112
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:17,500
and hand over to a human.

113
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:21,200
That's the real engineering.

114
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:23,800
So, what do we intend to do?

115
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:26,600
The goal is not another IVR,

116
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:29,800
not a menu that plays pre-recorded options.

117
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:32,600
It's a personal agent that understands

118
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:35,200
who's calling and why,

119
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:37,700
keeps context, answers questions,

120
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:40,500
takes a message, or checks your availability

121
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:42,500
and books a time—

122
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:45,900
all with the owner's permission.

123
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:49,300
The first milestone is a working cloud-based phone agent.

124
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:52,400
The bigger ambition is making it work

125
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:55,200
with the phone people already use.

126
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:58,800
And Android may still have a role.

127
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:01,800
App Functions is an Android platform API

128
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:04,100
with a Jetpack library

129
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:07,300
that lets an app expose its features as tools for AI agents.

130
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:09,800
Think of it as the mobile counterpart

131
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:12,600
to Model Context Protocol (MCP),

132
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:15,600
which connects agents to outside services.

133
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:18,500
App Functions needs Android 16 or higher,

134
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:20,900
and it's still early days.

135
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:23,800
They won't unlock cellular call audio,

136
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:26,700
but they could connect the agent to the rest

137
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:29,200
of the owner's digital life:

138
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:33,100
calendar, messages, notes, tasks.

139
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:35,500
We're not there yet.

140
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:38,400
This is where we're starting.

141
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:40,900
Now I want to know:

142
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:44,500
would you let an AI answer your phone calls for you?

143
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:47,700
Or is that a line you won't cross?

144
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:50,500
And if you've fought with Android telephony

145
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:53,400
or voice AI yourself,

146
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:55,700
tell me what I'm missing.

147
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:58,500
Drop it in the comments.
